
  A typical IPW Makefile for an application looks something like:

===========================================================
include $(IPW)/lib/make/Makestart
 
PGM= ipw2ppm
 
SRCS=\
        headers.c main.c ipwload.c initcolors.c readcolormap.c rgblookup.c \
        pgmwrite.c ppmwrite.c ipw3ppm.c
 
default: pgm
install: install-pgm
 
include $(IPW)/lib/make/Makerules
 
$(OBJS): pgm.h
===========================================================

  First, include Makestart.  Next come the information and definitions that
are relevent to your program (or library, directory, etc).  Then include
Makerules, and finally give the dependencies.  Note that the order that these
things are given is important.  You can change them if you know what you are
doing, but they have a logic to them.

  Makestart will include local and Makestd.  Here is a description of what
each file is intended to do:

  local:
          This file sets up the variables that are appropriate for your
        machine and O/S.  They define the compiler, compiler options, linker,
        archiver, lint, and some other commands.

  Makestd:
          This sets up various variables that shouldn't need to be changed.
        This includes how to run the C compiler (using the variables defined
        in local), library prefixes and suffixes, where programs and libraries
        are found within IPW, permissions that should be set, and how to make
        object files from C and Fortran code.

  Makerules:
          This is where the logic of make gets used.  This file tells make
        what commands get run when any rule is activated.  It also includes
        logic to do recursive makes on each directory.


Instructions for Make files
---------------------------

Variables:

  DIRS:		make will attempt to do a recursive make on each directory
		listed in this variable.  Make will run a depth-first algorithm
		on these directories, so be sure they are defined in the order
		you want them run.  For top-level directories, be sure to
		make your libraries before your binaries, i.e. define DIRS to
		be "lib etc bin" or equivalent.

  SCRIPT:	This is the name of the script that will be installed.  The
		script itself should have a .sh ending.
		Defining this variable tells make that you are making a script
		and not a library or program.

		NOT-DONE: You can make a script entitled script.awk or
		script.pl which would be a script written in AWK or Perl,
		and it will install these scripts if the language is available.
		In local, the preferred order is defined (default is to use
		Perl, AWK, then shell.

  SCRIPT_DIR:	This is where the script will get installed.  Normally you
		don't want to change this.

  LIB:		This defines the name of the resulting library that will be
		made.  Defining this variable tells make that you want to
		make a library.

  PGM:		The name of the program.

  SRCS:		This lists your source programs.  These are the programs that
		will be used to make your library or program.  The variable
		OBJS is automatically defined for you from this one, according
		to the standard rules for making an object file from source
		files (set up in Makestd).  In other words, if you have foo.c
		in SRCS, you will get foo.o in OBJS.

  SUBLIBS:	Define this to "NO_LIBS" to tell make that there are no
		libraries under this directory.  This will speed up the make
		process.

Targets:

  default:	This is what happens by default.  Usually for a library this
		is "lib", for a program "pgm", and for a script, "script".
		For directories, it is usually "all".

  install:	This is what happens when install is given as an argument to
		make.  This is usually "install-lib" for libraries, install-pgm
		for programs, and "install-script" for scripts.  Directories
		have "install-all" as their install action.

Hints:

  When making libraries that have header files in an h/ directory, set
C_SPECIAL to the directory where the headers live, e.g.:
          C_SPECIAL= -I$(IPW)/src/exp/erlc/h
right after you include Makestart.
